				<h2 id="linernotes">Liner notes</h2>
                
                <p>Written by Enya's lyricist, <a href="/roma-ryan-nicky/">Roma Ryan</a>, in <em>Only Time: The Collection</em>.</p>
                
                <h3>Watermark</h3>
                
                <p><em>Has the night taken from you
                    <br>the one who loves you?
                    
                    <br>No. Love is forever.
                    
                    <br>In your eyes is the sadness of one
                    
                    <br>who remembers their loss.
                    
                    <br>Love will not leave you.
                    
                    <br>
                    
                    <br>Love does not fall under a name or
                    
                    <br>the mark on a page.
                    
                    <br>Love is unencumbered,
                    
                    <br>it's free and it's pure and it's brave.
                    
                    <br>In your heart is the island
                    
                    <br>where memories wash on the shore.
                    
                    <br>Love is an ocean.
                    
                    <br>Has the night taken from you
                    
                    <br>the one who belongs to you?
                    
                    <br>
                    
                    <br>
                    
                    No. Love is forever.</em></p>
                
                <p>The title of the track from the album "Watermark" was
                    inspired by a poem I had written at that time. It remained as a
                    poem and was not adapted for lyric. This is the first time the poem
                    has been in print.</p>

                <p>The track was later dedicated and used for the official tribute album
                    for <strong>Princess Diana</strong>, the proceeds of which went directly to the memorial fund
                    set up in her name after she expressed her personal fondness for the piece.</p>
                
                <h3>Boadicea</h3>
                
                <p><strong>Boadicea</strong> or <strong>Boudicca</strong>, meaning "Victorious," was the Queen of the
                    <strong>Iceni tribe</strong> of <strong>East Anglia</strong>.</p>

                <p>She led a fierce rebellion in <strong>60 A.D.</strong> against
                    the <strong>Romans</strong>, destroying the cities of <strong>Colchester</strong>, <strong>St. Albans</strong>,
                    and capturing <strong>London</strong>.</p>

                <p>She was eventually defeated by the Romans,
                    and rather than be humiliated by them, she poisoned herself.</p>
                
                <h3>Storms in Africa</h3>
                
                <p>There are two recorded versions of <strong>"Storms in Africa"</strong>—one sung in Gaelic and one in English.</p>

                <p>The Gaelic version is our personal favourite, which we have included here on this collection after both versions were released as singles in <strong>1989</strong>.</p>
                
                <h3>The Celts</h3>
                
                <p>This was the main title theme for the <strong>BBC</strong> television series. There were six
                    programmes in the series with <strong>72 minutes</strong> of original music recorded.</p>

                <p>The director incorporated various pastiches in the programme and requested music to
                    complement them, ranging from a <strong>"Spaghetti Western"</strong> to <em>The Third Man</em> and <em>The
                    Haunted House</em>.</p>

                <p>What happened to the remaining unreleased tracks? We still have them in our studio vaults!</p>
                
                <h3>Orinoco Flow</h3>
                
                <p>Although it was the last track written for the album <em>Watermark</em>,
                    it was chosen as the lead single and met with monumental success, reaching <strong>number 1</strong> in the United Kingdom.</p>

                <p>There was a profound sense of discovery in creating this album—a
                    sense of new beginnings and adventure that <strong>"Orinoco Flow"</strong> perfectly captures for us.</p>
                
                <h3>Ebudae</h3>
                
                <p><strong>Ebudae</strong>, Latin for the <strong>Hebrides</strong> islands in Scotland, is referred to in Ariosto's
                    <em>Orlando Furioso</em> as the <strong>Dreadful Isle</strong> or the <strong>Isle of Tears</strong>.</p>

                <p>It is also home to traditional Scottish <strong>"waulking songs,"</strong> the rhythm of which served as direct inspiration for this piece.</p>

                <p><strong>"Waulking songs"</strong> originate from the heritage of women weaving and chanting to the unrelenting rhythm of wool looms.</p>
                
                <h3>Book of Days</h3>
                
                <p><strong>"Book of Days"</strong> was adapted from its original Gaelic instrumental form
                    on <em>Shepherd Moons</em> for director <strong>Ron Howard's</strong> film <em>Far and Away</em>, starring <strong>Tom Cruise</strong> and <strong>Nicole Kidman</strong>.</p>

                <p>The English lyrics were rewritten around the core cinematic themes of the film.</p>
                
                <h3>Shepherd Moons</h3>
                
                <p>In <strong>1980</strong>, the <strong>Voyager</strong> space probe transmitted pictures to Earth revealing two tiny new moons orbiting the outer F ring of <strong>Saturn</strong>.</p>

                <p>Numbered <strong>1980S26</strong> and <strong>1980S27</strong>, these shepherd moons work ceaselessly on either side of the ring to keep cosmic particles together like a shepherd guiding a flock.</p>
                
                <h3>Caribbean Blue</h3>
                
                <p>Like <strong>Afer Ventus</strong> (the wind from Africa) or <strong>Zephyrus</strong> (the gentle west wind), human imagination is free to chart its own voyage.</p>

                <p>Released as a hit single in <strong>November 1991</strong>, <strong>"Caribbean Blue"</strong> represents the pursuit of an ideal that can be summarized in three words: <strong>believe in yourself</strong>.</p>
                
                <h3>Marble Halls</h3>
                
                <p>An operatic piece originally scored by the Irish composer and baritone <strong>Michael William Balfe</strong> (1808–1870) with lyrics by <strong>Alfred Bunn</strong>.</p>

                <p>Also known as "The Dream," it comes from the 1843 opera <em>The Bohemian Girl</em> and was praised by <strong>Johann Strauss</strong>, who called Balfe a "Master of Melody."</p>

                <p>This ambient adaptation was later featured in director <strong>Martin Scorsese's</strong> 1993 film <em>The Age of Innocence</em>.</p>
                
                <h3>The Memory of Trees</h3>
                
                <p>In ancient <strong>Celtic belief</strong>, trees held sacred spirits within them and served as guardians of memory and lore.</p>

                <p>The <strong>Druids</strong> recorded their wisdom using a secret alphabet called <strong>Ogham</strong>, named after <strong>Ogma</strong>, the God of poetry and eloquence.</p>

                <p>With the modern destruction of the world's rainforests, the title takes on a bittersweet meaning—where trees may soon become nothing more than a memory.</p>
                
                <h3>Anywhere Is</h3>
                
                <p>A spirited single released in <strong>1995</strong> that explores life's unexpected journeys.</p>

                <p>Well, it is, isn't it?</p>
                
                <h3>China Roses</h3>
                
                <p>Everyone possesses a unique vision of heaven created by memories, wishes, and desires—from a changing sky to the crimson flower of the keys of heaven.</p>

                <p>This personal sanctuary is reflected in the Latin quotation: <em>Denique non omnes eadem mirantur amantque</em> (All men, in short, do not admire and love the same things).</p>
                
